## 7. Clean replay: what the product can and cannot guarantee [CHOICE]

**Guaranteed by a clean replay** (fresh kernel, epoch bump, DAG order, declared inputs only):
- the executed notebook is a **separate artifact** from the source notebook (nbclient-style [S15]); source and results are never conflated;
- outputs are bound to: exact cell source hashes, dataset identity digests, environment lock hash, engine version, epoch, run id;
- per-cell progress, cancel, timeout, and error capture (timeout and error hooks exist in nbclient [S15]);
- deterministic-vs-stable ordering discipline: replay sorts carry explicit content tie-breaks, and comparison uses manifest digests (§5.3) [EXECUTED W2];
- the manifest invariants (kept+quarantined==total; digests recomputed at export).

**Explicitly outside the guarantee** (each replay-eligible but not guaranteed identical): wall-clock/time-dependent code; randomness; unordered iterations whose order varies across versions; unstable sorts without declared tie-break; external network services; undeclared filesystem access; unpinned packages (lock hash mismatch makes the manifest self-declaring as not-reproducible); hardware/BLAS-sensitive numerics; locale/collation differences. The manifest carries an `exclusions` list, and a "reproduced bit-identically" claim is only made when both digests match [CHOICE].

